# Kotlin Gray-Part — Launch Flow & Attribution Contract

Everything here was paid for with failed builds on a real device. The routing
tree in `kotlin_gray_guide.mdc` says *what* the app decides; this file says
*when* each part of the machinery may run, and it is the part that goes wrong.

The single sentence to keep: **attribution is asked for exactly once per
install, and the app only gets one chance to ask it properly.** Every bug below
is some variation of asking too early, asking from the wrong place, or writing
down an answer that was never given.

---

## 1. Starting AppsFlyer: two strokes, never one

`init` and `start` do different jobs and belong in different places.

| Call | Where | Why there |
|---|---|---|
| `subscribeForDeepLink` + `init(key, listener, appContext)` | `Application.onCreate` | Registers the conversion listener and the activity-lifecycle callbacks the SDK uses to notice the app is in the foreground. Sends nothing. |
| `start(activity)` | Router activity, **after** connectivity is confirmed | This is the call that puts the install on the wire and triggers the conversion callback. |

```kotlin
// Application.onCreate — introductions only, not a byte on the wire.
fun prime() {
    if (primed) return
    primed = true
    val key = Blueprint.trackerKey()
    if (key.isBlank()) { finish(emptyMap()); deepLink.complete(Unit); return }
    runCatching {
        AppsFlyerLib.getInstance().apply {
            subscribeForDeepLink(deepLinkListener)   // before init, always
            init(key, conversionListener, ctx.applicationContext)
        }
    }.onFailure { finish(emptyMap()); deepLink.complete(Unit) }
}

// Router, once a link is confirmed. `host` is the Activity, and that matters.
fun ignite(host: Activity) {
    prime()
    if (started || Blueprint.trackerKey().isBlank()) return
    started = true
    runCatching { AppsFlyerLib.getInstance().start(host) }
        .onFailure { finish(emptyMap()); deepLink.complete(Unit) }
}
```

### Why `init` cannot move out of the Application

The SDK finds out that the app is in the foreground from
`ActivityLifecycleCallbacks` it registers during `init`. Register them while an
activity is **already resumed** and the SDK misses the event: it queues the
launch and sends it at the *next* activity transition. In a gray app the next
activity is the native game — which means the conversion arrives roughly half a
minute later, long after the routing decision was made and written down.

Measured on device, with `init`+`start` both called from the router:

```
11:57:58  AppsFlyer started
          ...30 s of nothing...
11:58:28  conversion={}                        ← gave up, went native
11:58:30  onBecameForeground → "Sending first launch for this session!"
11:58:34  onConversionDataSuccess: af_status=Non-organic   ← the right answer, 6 s too late
```

The SDK's own telemetry named the gap: `"first_launch":{"init_to_fg":32239}`.

### Why `start` must be handed the Activity

AppsFlyer's integration guide, verbatim: *"If the app calls `start` from an
Activity, it should pass the Activity Context to the SDK. Failing to pass the
activity context will not trigger the SDK, thus losing attribution data and
in-app events."* `start(applicationContext)` from inside an activity is the
same 30-second hang as above.

This split is AppsFlyer's own documented "deferred start" pattern, the one
written for consent dialogs. A connectivity gate is the same shape of problem,
so the same shape of solution applies.

---

## 2. Never start AppsFlyer without a link

An SDK started offline reaches for its servers, misses, and calls
`onConversionDataFail` within milliseconds. That empty answer is **final for
the session**: a `CompletableDeferred` completes once, and the SDK will not
redo a conversion it considers settled. The user then turns Wi-Fi on, the
router asks again, gets the cached empty map back in two milliseconds, sends a
body with no attribution, and the backend — correctly — answers 404. A paid
install is filed as organic and, if the mode is persisted, stays that way for
the life of the install.

The rule: **the connectivity check runs before `start`, always, on every path.**
Nothing else in the launch may reorder those two.

Defence in depth, in the order they should be tried:

1. **Gate.** `start` only after connectivity is confirmed. Fixes the cause.
2. **Re-ask.** If a previous attempt completed *empty*, replace the deferred
   and call `start(activity)` again. Guard it so an attempt still in flight is
   left alone and a non-empty answer (organic counts as an answer) is kept —
   otherwise a healthy launch sends two launch events.
3. **Ask AppsFlyer directly.** When the SDK still has nothing:

   ```
   GET <gcdBase><bundleId>?devkey=<devKey>&device_id=<uid>
   ```

   The dev key is a **query parameter**. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer` it is
   ignored and every call comes back `400 {"error_reason":"The 'devkey' query
   parameter is not found"}` — a fallback that silently never fires, which is
   worse than not having one. See pitfalls #37.

   Give the SDK a few seconds first: GCD also answers `400` for an install
   AppsFlyer has not been told about yet, and an install queued while offline
   is only filed once the launch goes out. **Log the response body**, not just
   the status — it is the only thing that tells those two 400s apart.

```kotlin
fun retrace(host: Activity) {
    if (!started) { ignite(host); return }
    val last = settled ?: return          // still in flight — leave it
    if (last.isNotEmpty()) return         // has an answer — keep it
    settled = null
    reasked = true
    reaskedAt = System.currentTimeMillis()
    conversion = CompletableDeferred()
    runCatching { AppsFlyerLib.getInstance().start(host) }
}

suspend fun awaitConversion(timeoutMs: Long): Map<String, Any?> {
    // A repeat question gets less time: the SDK may never call back at all.
    val window = if (reasked) minOf(timeoutMs, 8_000L) else timeoutMs
    val fromSdk = withTimeoutOrNull(window) { conversion.await() } ?: emptyMap()
    if (fromSdk.isNotEmpty()) return fromSdk
    if (reasked) {
        val waited = System.currentTimeMillis() - reaskedAt
        if (waited < 8_000L) delay(8_000L - waited)
    }
    return askGcd()?.takeIf { it.isNotEmpty() }?.also { settled = it } ?: fromSdk
}
```

