## 5. What may be persisted as the mode

A negative verdict sticks for the life of the install, so it has to be earned.
Persist `NATIVE` only when **both** hold:

- the endpoint actually **answered** — 404, empty body, `ok:false`, anything
  with an HTTP status. A thrown request, a DNS failure or a missing endpoint
  is not an answer;
- the request carried **real attribution**. An empty conversion map means the
  endpoint was told nothing about where the install came from, so its "no"
  is about the request, not about the user.

Otherwise: open the game for this run, log why, and leave the mode unset so the
next launch asks again. Distinguishing the two kinds of "no" needs a flag on
the response model — the config client cannot collapse a transport failure and
a 404 into the same value:

```kotlin
data class ChannelResult(
    val active: Boolean,
    val destination: String?,
    val expiresAt: Long,
    val answered: Boolean          // false only when nobody replied
)

when {
    !result.answered  -> log("endpoint unreachable -> game, decision left open")
    attribution.isEmpty() -> log("no attribution behind the answer -> game, decision left open")
    else -> vault.mode = MODE_NATIVE
}
goNative()
```

This is the one deliberate exception to "the answer sticks", and it is worth
the handful of extra requests it can cause in rare cases: the alternative is
locking a paid user out of the gray part forever because of one bad second of
network.

